{"statement":"For $N\in\mathbb N$ let $r_3(N)$ denote the largest size of a set $A\subseteq[N]=\{1,\dots,N\}$ containing no non-trivial $3$-term arithmetic progression, i.e. no $x,x+d,x+2d\in A$ with $d\neq0$. Behrend's construction gives $r_3(N)\ge N\exp\big(-(c_B+o(1))\sqrt{\log N}\big)$ with $c_B=2\sqrt{2\log 2}\approx 2.355$, and no construction has improved this constant. Define
$$\kappa=\liminf_{N\to\infty}\frac{\log\big(N/r_3(N)\big)}{\sqrt{\log N}}\in[0,c_B].$$
Problem. Construct an infinite family of $3$-AP-free sets $A_N\subseteq[N]$ with $|A_N|\ge N\exp(-c\sqrt{\log N})$ for some constant $c<2\sqrt{2\log 2}$, thereby showing $\kappa<c_B$. More ambitiously: is $\kappa=0$, i.e. are there $3$-AP-free sets with $|A_N|\ge N\exp(-(\log N)^{\gamma})$ for some fixed $\gamma<1/2$ and infinitely many $N$?","grounding":"This extends the section "Result: Strong bounds for 3-term progressions". Kelley–Meka prove the upper bound $r_3(N)\le N\exp(-c(\log N)^{\beta})$ for a small absolute $\beta>0$, while the only lower bound is Behrend's $N\exp(-(2\sqrt{2\log 2}+o(1))\sqrt{\log N})$ (Elkin's refinement changes only a polylogarithmic factor, not the constant in the exponent). The gap left open is the entire lower-bound side: nothing in the section (or in the literature) rules out either $\kappa=c_B$ or $\kappa=0$, and the exponent $1/2$ versus $\beta$ is precisely where the upper and lower bounds disagree. The problem asks for the construction/extremal-object side of that gap. The finite instance below is a concrete window ($N=20000$) where neither Behrend-type constructions nor exhaustive search (exact values of $r_3(N)$ are known only for $N$ of order a few hundred) determine the answer.","why_hard":"(1) Behrend-type constructions (sphere level sets in a high-dimensional box, base-$(2m)$ digit embedding) lose a factor $2^{d}$ from restricting digits to half the range and a factor $dm^{2}$ from pigeonholing over radii; optimising $d$ gives exactly the constant $2\sqrt{2\log 2}$. Elkin and Green–Wolf squeeze the pigeonhole loss down to $\sqrt{\log N}$ by using thin annuli, but the $2^{d}$ digit loss is structural (it prevents carries), so the constant is unchanged. (2) Replacing the sphere by other convex bodies or by sets with no three collinear points in $\{0,\dots,m-1\}^{d}$: the best known collinearity-free sets in such grids are again of relative size $\exp(-\Theta(d))$ after the digit restriction, so nothing beyond $c_B$ is obtained; even the strongest known results for the analogous finite-field model (caps in $\mathbb F_3^{n}$, where the extremal exponent is unknown) do not transfer to the integers without a digit embedding that reintroduces the $2^{d}$ loss. (3) Probabilistic/algebraic constructions (random sets, Salem–Spencer digit sets, Stanley greedy sequences) give only $N^{1-\delta}$. (4) Computer search gives excellent sets for $N\le$ a few hundred but the search space is $2^{N}$ and no local-search heuristic is known to reach even the Behrend regime for large $N$, so the finite instance resists brute force. (5) On the impossibility side, no upper-bound technique (density increment, almost-periodicity, Kelley–Meka sifting) can distinguish exponent $1/2$ from exponent $\beta$, so even showing $\kappa>0$ is out of reach.","why_important":"The exponent of $\log N$ in $\log(N/r_3(N))$ is the central remaining question for Roth's theorem: Kelley–Meka pushed the upper bound to $\exp(-c(\log N)^{\beta})$ and it is widely suspected that Behrend's $1/2$ is the truth, but there is no evidence for this beyond the failure of attempts to beat Behrend. Any improvement of the constant $c_B$ would be the first progress on the lower bound since 1946 and would reveal new structure in large progression-free sets; a proof that $\kappa<c_B$ via a non-sphere construction would also feed into the corona-type lower bounds for $r_k(N)$ (Rankin, O'Bryant) and into lower bounds for arithmetic Ramsey numbers. Conversely, if $\kappa=0$ were achieved, the Kelley–Meka exponent $\beta$ would no longer be obviously far from optimal, changing the target of the whole upper-bound programme.","resembles_known":"Behrend's construction / the Erdős–Turán problem on $r_3(N)$ (lower-bound side of Roth's theorem)","novelty_confidence":0.6,"finite_form":"Find a set $A\subseteq\{1,2,\dots,20000\}$ with $|A|\ge1300$ containing no three distinct elements $x<y<z$ with $x+z=2y$. (For comparison: the digit construction with base-$3$ digits in $\{0,1\}$ gives about $520$ elements, the product of exact extremal sets for $[123]$ and $[81]$ gives $32\cdot24=768$ elements in $[19926]$, and extrapolating the known exact values $r_3(N)$ for $N\le123$ with the empirical local growth exponent $\approx0.70$–$0.75$ places $r_3(20000)$ somewhere around $1100$–$1500$; $1300$ is believed to be beyond any published or easily searched construction.)","candidate_format":"A JSON array of at least 1300 distinct integers, each between 1 and 20000 inclusive (order irrelevant).","checker_code":"def check(candidate) -> bool:
